1
00:00:01,912 --> 00:00:03,622
Hi everyone, and welcome.

2
00:00:04,931 --> 00:00:06,272
My name is Bhasker Udaykiran.

3
00:00:06,782 --> 00:00:08,562
I'm Staff Performance
Engineer at ServiceNow.

4
00:00:10,532 --> 00:00:15,411
And for years, I lived in one surprisingly
hard question: When the next traffic hits

5
00:00:15,411 --> 00:00:20,112
like Black Friday, a product launch, or
a big sale day, will the system hold?

6
00:00:20,732 --> 00:00:23,871
The usual way we answer that is
load test, and the load testing

7
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:25,011
is a whole problem, right?

8
00:00:25,421 --> 00:00:29,671
We have great tools but here what I
learned, the tool was never the weak link.

9
00:00:29,841 --> 00:00:31,371
The weak link is the workload model.

10
00:00:31,851 --> 00:00:35,271
The assumption that test is built
on, like the number of virtual users,

11
00:00:35,591 --> 00:00:39,301
the request per second, the mix
of user journey, and the duration.

12
00:00:39,751 --> 00:00:44,101
If those inputs are guesses, a beautifully
ex-executed test will give you confidence

13
00:00:44,111 --> 00:00:45,571
for a well-dressed wrong answers.

14
00:00:46,031 --> 00:00:49,201
So today I'll show you how to stop
guessing and how to build a workload

15
00:00:49,211 --> 00:00:52,572
model where every number is derived
directly from the observability

16
00:00:52,572 --> 00:00:54,141
data you already collected.

17
00:00:54,671 --> 00:00:59,381
Here's the line to hold on: Peak readiness
should be computed, not negotiated.

18
00:00:59,881 --> 00:01:00,361
Let's get into it

19
00:01:01,361 --> 00:01:03,831
The most expensive word in
peak readiness is estimate.

20
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:05,221
I've seen this many times.

21
00:01:05,611 --> 00:01:09,941
A few weeks before the peak season, we get
into a room and plan for the load test.

22
00:01:10,201 --> 00:01:14,211
Someone asks how many concurrent users
should we model, and what follows

23
00:01:14,211 --> 00:01:16,051
isn't analysis, it's negotiation.

24
00:01:16,401 --> 00:01:18,721
We pick round numbers because
round number feels safe.

25
00:01:19,021 --> 00:01:21,721
We run the test, it passes,
everyone feels relaxed.

26
00:01:22,111 --> 00:01:24,671
Then the real peak arrives, and
the system falls over anyway.

27
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:27,641
And the reason is almost
never the test was too weak.

28
00:01:28,311 --> 00:01:30,731
It produced the test in the wrong shape.

29
00:01:31,361 --> 00:01:35,511
Let's example we targeted for the checkout
heavy when the real peak was browse heavy.

30
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:38,741
Or sized it for an average
that hid in one-minute spike.

31
00:01:39,231 --> 00:01:43,011
A model built on assumptions can produce
a perfect test of wrong workload model,

32
00:01:43,631 --> 00:01:47,461
and that-that's the most dangerous outcome
because it comes wearing a check mark.

33
00:01:48,071 --> 00:01:50,861
Doing this by hand is almost
thirteen to twenty-four hours

34
00:01:50,891 --> 00:01:52,241
across two to four people.

35
00:01:52,771 --> 00:01:56,341
The automated approach I'll show you
under five minutes with one engineer.

36
00:01:57,001 --> 00:01:59,031
About a ninety-nine percent cut in effort.

37
00:01:59,491 --> 00:02:00,861
But the goal is not speed.

38
00:02:01,201 --> 00:02:04,521
The goal is to replace opinion
with evidence and keep that

39
00:02:04,521 --> 00:02:05,801
evidence attached to the model.

40
00:02:06,241 --> 00:02:07,301
Speed is the bonus.

41
00:02:07,671 --> 00:02:11,861
Traceability is the point The manual
approach fails because it's not one

42
00:02:11,861 --> 00:02:16,081
decision, it's a chain of fragile ones,
and a chain break at weakest link.

43
00:02:16,481 --> 00:02:20,411
You scroll graphs and export CVS to
find the peak, and your eye missed it.

44
00:02:20,511 --> 00:02:22,511
You pivot hourly data to pick the window.

45
00:02:22,931 --> 00:02:25,501
You interview teams and turn
opinions into scenario mix.

46
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:28,811
Four to eight hours of guessed
work dressed up as a model.

47
00:02:29,251 --> 00:02:31,651
You calculate feature users
in a spreadsheet, then you

48
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:33,061
document and chase sign off.

49
00:02:33,501 --> 00:02:35,681
Altogether, add it up
as a full sprint week.

50
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:38,151
But the real cost isn't hours.

51
00:02:38,811 --> 00:02:40,131
It's a quiet failure mode.

52
00:02:40,511 --> 00:02:44,441
The test can come green when
workload model is completely wrong.

53
00:02:45,311 --> 00:02:46,451
A failing test is a gift.

54
00:02:46,611 --> 00:02:48,371
It tells you to go fix something.

55
00:02:48,781 --> 00:02:51,531
But a green test and the wrong
workload model hands you a false

56
00:02:51,561 --> 00:02:55,121
confidence, and false confidence
is exactly what breaks on peak day.

57
00:02:55,661 --> 00:03:01,091
Everything nasty is to build to kill that
one failure mode Here's the key idea.

58
00:03:01,521 --> 00:03:03,931
Treat your observed data as
a workload specification.

59
00:03:04,391 --> 00:03:08,921
We already track everything like
requests, sessions, latency, traces,

60
00:03:08,951 --> 00:03:13,561
user journey, and we use it to figure out
what went wrong after something breaks.

61
00:03:13,971 --> 00:03:14,931
The insight is simple.

62
00:03:15,281 --> 00:03:18,321
The same telemetric that tells you
what happens can tell you what to

63
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:20,081
reproduce before it happens again.

64
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:23,061
The spec for your next load
test is already in your system.

65
00:03:23,581 --> 00:03:25,471
You ha-- just have to
ask the right questions.

66
00:03:25,911 --> 00:03:27,271
The method is three moves.

67
00:03:27,441 --> 00:03:31,711
We observe the raw signals, we derive
the four things the test need like peak

68
00:03:31,711 --> 00:03:36,391
RPS, concurrency, scenario weight, and
duration, and we generate a model where

69
00:03:36,391 --> 00:03:38,341
every number carries its source query.

70
00:03:39,651 --> 00:03:41,291
And this is not tied to one tool.

71
00:03:41,841 --> 00:03:46,431
I'll use NRQL language for this task,
but Dynatrace does the same thing

72
00:03:46,431 --> 00:03:51,081
with DQL, and so does anything exposes
transactions in tel-- session telemetrics.

73
00:03:51,641 --> 00:03:55,701
It's a methodology, not a product
The method is six-step drill down.

74
00:03:56,071 --> 00:04:00,591
We start with a wide window of history,
narrow to single worst window, then

75
00:04:00,601 --> 00:04:02,321
build the workload model around it.

76
00:04:02,681 --> 00:04:05,221
Start broad, usually the
last year's peak season.

77
00:04:05,691 --> 00:04:09,301
Find the peak day, inside that peak
hour, inside that the peak minute,

78
00:04:09,771 --> 00:04:11,631
the worst 60 seconds in the data set.

79
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:16,371
Then step four through step six, turn that
window into scenario mix, the concurrent

80
00:04:16,391 --> 00:04:17,961
users, and the session population.

81
00:04:19,451 --> 00:04:22,721
One word on the growth factor,
because you should push on it.

82
00:04:23,151 --> 00:04:26,241
We derive last year's peaks
from the data, then apply

83
00:04:26,261 --> 00:04:27,671
expert multiplier for this year.

84
00:04:28,831 --> 00:04:29,841
I don't invent those.

85
00:04:29,911 --> 00:04:32,771
I anchor them to my own
year-over-year growth.

86
00:04:33,511 --> 00:04:36,411
Twenty to 30% means one
point two to one point three.

87
00:04:36,761 --> 00:04:38,821
A big marketing push means one point five.

88
00:04:39,211 --> 00:04:40,691
And 2X isn't a forecast.

89
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:43,821
It's a deliberate stress
to find where things break.

90
00:04:44,691 --> 00:04:49,961
The formula is target RPS equals
peak RPS times growth times

91
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:51,331
one point five safety buffer.

92
00:04:51,791 --> 00:04:56,411
And every multiplier says visible and
separate, so you can always tell what

93
00:04:56,411 --> 00:04:58,091
was observed and what was assumed.

94
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:02,771
So here's peak day, peak hour,
peak minute, scenario mix,

95
00:05:02,791 --> 00:05:04,061
concurrent users, sessions.

96
00:05:04,631 --> 00:05:09,031
Let's walk each one If there is one
technical idea to take from this

97
00:05:09,031 --> 00:05:14,011
talk, it's this: Don't size from
a average, find the worst minute.

98
00:05:14,551 --> 00:05:19,211
Team divides a busy day request by hour,
get an average RPS, and build around it.

99
00:05:19,691 --> 00:05:21,361
But averages are comforting lies.

100
00:05:21,551 --> 00:05:24,121
They smooth away the
spikes the system downs.

101
00:05:24,451 --> 00:05:27,881
Your infrastructure doesn't fail on
average, it fails on the worst one.

102
00:05:28,391 --> 00:05:29,871
So we'll drill down the peak day.

103
00:05:30,051 --> 00:05:32,431
In this example, November
28th, which has one hundred and

104
00:05:32,431 --> 00:05:33,851
fifty-two thousand requests.

105
00:05:34,331 --> 00:05:37,881
Peak hour is around to twelve, and peak
minute is around twenty-three, which

106
00:05:37,881 --> 00:05:39,471
has eighty-two requests per second.

107
00:05:40,561 --> 00:05:42,571
That minute is the number that matters.

108
00:05:42,751 --> 00:05:45,341
Everything before it just
narrows the search to find it.

109
00:05:46,161 --> 00:05:48,541
Let me show you a real
query and a real result.

110
00:05:50,431 --> 00:05:51,371
Here is the step one.

111
00:05:51,831 --> 00:05:54,671
We can see the query on the top
and the result is the below.

112
00:05:55,281 --> 00:05:57,401
And a query is reproducible and auditable.

113
00:05:57,841 --> 00:05:58,461
It's simple.

114
00:05:58,731 --> 00:06:02,821
For each day, over the last thirty
days, we count transactions and pull

115
00:06:02,821 --> 00:06:07,221
requests per minute, average and
sample response time and error count.

116
00:06:07,661 --> 00:06:09,291
Group by day, order by volume.

117
00:06:09,961 --> 00:06:14,251
For example, November twentieth
is the clear peak, a hundred and

118
00:06:14,251 --> 00:06:17,051
fifty-two thousand requests with
the twenty-eight thousand sessions.

119
00:06:18,491 --> 00:06:23,551
One caution, clear inputs like filtered
out bots and synthetic traffic, watch

120
00:06:23,551 --> 00:06:25,631
for sampling and lock your time zone.

121
00:06:25,791 --> 00:06:30,521
Because if step one uses a different
clock than step two, everything drifts.

122
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:33,501
And here's the key.

123
00:06:33,761 --> 00:06:36,451
This exact window feeds
every query that follows.

124
00:06:37,231 --> 00:06:41,571
If it is wrong, everything downstream
will be wrong, which is why we query

125
00:06:41,571 --> 00:06:47,341
it instead of guessing it Step two,
bucket the peak day by hour, count

126
00:06:47,351 --> 00:06:51,091
request, grab P95 latency, group by hour.

127
00:06:51,151 --> 00:06:55,171
For example, the busiest is eleven to
twelve, which has twenty-nine thousand

128
00:06:55,171 --> 00:07:00,091
request, forty-two hundred sessions,
and P95 of eight twenty milliseconds.

129
00:07:01,141 --> 00:07:03,991
Why the hour when we size the
throughput from the minute?

130
00:07:04,921 --> 00:07:06,711
Because it answers a different question.

131
00:07:07,501 --> 00:07:10,481
Is your peak a sustained
plateau or a single spike?

132
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:14,651
This s-- this one sustained
volume, sessions, and latency

133
00:07:14,701 --> 00:07:15,751
all elevated together.

134
00:07:17,251 --> 00:07:21,491
So your test must hold the load for
the full hour, which surfaces the

135
00:07:21,491 --> 00:07:23,431
slow problems a burst never will.

136
00:07:24,681 --> 00:07:28,931
That's your test duration One caution,
especially for single-page apps,

137
00:07:29,371 --> 00:07:33,371
use browser interaction events, not
page views, and know your session

138
00:07:33,371 --> 00:07:37,201
timeouts because sessions become
the basis for sizing virtual users

139
00:07:37,221 --> 00:07:42,021
next Step three, the peak made into
a defensible throughput target.

140
00:07:42,571 --> 00:07:43,981
This is a slide I would frame.

141
00:07:44,631 --> 00:07:47,311
The query is one-minute
time series across the time.

142
00:07:48,181 --> 00:07:53,591
At 11:23, we took around 4,920
request divided by 60, which is

143
00:07:53,851 --> 00:07:56,601
around eight-- 82 requests per second.

144
00:07:57,061 --> 00:07:57,841
That's not a guess.

145
00:07:57,871 --> 00:07:58,601
That happened.

146
00:07:59,831 --> 00:08:02,711
Then we apply growth,
the safety out of it.

147
00:08:03,381 --> 00:08:09,661
82 times 1.3 times 1.1 gives you a
ceiling of 114 requests per second,

148
00:08:10,431 --> 00:08:11,881
and that's the whole philosophy.

149
00:08:12,361 --> 00:08:16,291
When someone asks why 114,
not a round number of 100,

150
00:08:16,911 --> 00:08:18,301
the answer isn't feel right.

151
00:08:18,551 --> 00:08:19,891
It's observed number.

152
00:08:20,951 --> 00:08:23,661
The growth assumption and the
safety margin, each one on the

153
00:08:23,661 --> 00:08:24,991
table and each one arguable.

154
00:08:25,931 --> 00:08:27,351
That's what makes it defensible.

155
00:08:28,861 --> 00:08:33,221
And one loop that turns this from
a nice idea to proven one, it

156
00:08:33,231 --> 00:08:35,271
doesn't end when you build a model.

157
00:08:35,591 --> 00:08:37,011
It's end when you validate it.

158
00:08:37,771 --> 00:08:40,571
After the real peak, run
the same query against what

159
00:08:40,571 --> 00:08:42,571
actually happened and compare.

160
00:08:43,551 --> 00:08:45,701
Did throughput land near the 114?

161
00:08:46,231 --> 00:08:51,571
That, that check will tells you whether
your test was harnessed, and it hand you

162
00:08:51,571 --> 00:08:53,331
the real growth number for the next year.

163
00:08:54,441 --> 00:08:58,481
So derive, test, and then
check yourself against reality.

164
00:09:00,011 --> 00:09:04,601
Step four, the scenario mix, where
manual model goes mostly wrong

165
00:09:04,801 --> 00:09:07,791
because weights are usually just
opinions from the planning meeting.

166
00:09:08,501 --> 00:09:11,291
Someone says user mostly
search and do some checkouts.

167
00:09:12,001 --> 00:09:16,591
Instead of that, we can query what
actually users did in the peak hour, and

168
00:09:16,591 --> 00:09:18,331
each percentage become a scenario weight.

169
00:09:18,811 --> 00:09:21,351
For example, in this case,
homepage to browse, which is

170
00:09:21,451 --> 00:09:24,891
28 percentage and checkout to
payment is only six percentage.

171
00:09:25,861 --> 00:09:26,881
Those are not guesses.

172
00:09:26,961 --> 00:09:29,891
That's production telling you
the truth from about itself.

173
00:09:30,671 --> 00:09:34,771
And we cross-check from three
angles like transaction volume,

174
00:09:34,921 --> 00:09:36,011
top routes, and the funnel.

175
00:09:37,011 --> 00:09:41,301
One caution to point, normalize your
routes so coordinate limit doesn't

176
00:09:41,311 --> 00:09:46,341
truncate results and rederive the
mix every cycle because weight drifts

177
00:09:46,371 --> 00:09:50,501
as the future and campaign changes,
and rerunning a query is free.

178
00:09:51,771 --> 00:09:56,051
Here's the key point: Weight equals
observed share, not stakeholder

179
00:09:56,051 --> 00:09:59,121
preference Step five, concurrency.

180
00:09:59,661 --> 00:10:01,041
And there is a trap in the word.

181
00:10:01,541 --> 00:10:04,391
A virtual user is a whole
person on journey, not the

182
00:10:04,421 --> 00:10:06,181
number of HTTP questions fired.

183
00:10:06,631 --> 00:10:08,511
Confuse the two, you'll
size the test wrong.

184
00:10:09,041 --> 00:10:11,911
So we size from the session
concurrency, which is average

185
00:10:11,911 --> 00:10:16,571
sessions times average session
duration in seconds divided by 3,600.

186
00:10:16,571 --> 00:10:21,321
In this case, we have around 4,200
sessions at 440 seconds gives

187
00:10:21,321 --> 00:10:23,581
you 529 active users at once.

188
00:10:24,111 --> 00:10:30,751
Apply the growth and it will become
around 688 virtual users simultaneously.

189
00:10:31,371 --> 00:10:34,891
On the other hand, transition
concurrency, which is little slower at

190
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:39,391
request level is real and useful, but
it answers a different question for

191
00:10:39,641 --> 00:10:41,351
sizing thread and connection pools.

192
00:10:41,841 --> 00:10:44,701
Session concurrency answers
how many users are navigating.

193
00:10:45,181 --> 00:10:48,001
So don't use one where
you need the other one.

194
00:10:49,711 --> 00:10:52,241
Step six, keep two numbers separate.

195
00:10:52,741 --> 00:10:56,741
First one is simultaneous
concurrency, 529 active users at once.

196
00:10:57,311 --> 00:11:00,111
That what drives how many
virtual users hammers the system.

197
00:11:00,771 --> 00:11:05,391
The second one is session population,
4,280 distinct sessions across the hour.

198
00:11:06,381 --> 00:11:08,821
That's your data pool, the
ceiling of unique users.

199
00:11:09,751 --> 00:11:11,421
So here's how people get burned.

200
00:11:11,961 --> 00:11:16,601
They configured 4,280 users, run
a test eight times heavier than

201
00:11:16,631 --> 00:11:21,031
reality, and spend a week fixing a
problem that only exists in the test.

202
00:11:21,691 --> 00:11:23,801
So concurrency drives your active users.

203
00:11:24,291 --> 00:11:26,001
The session population
drives your data pool.

204
00:11:26,781 --> 00:11:28,021
Two questions, two numbers.

205
00:11:28,491 --> 00:11:33,331
We should not mix them Now let's go past
transactions to act-actual user journey.

206
00:11:33,921 --> 00:11:36,021
This is the richest thing
session telemetrics gives

207
00:11:36,021 --> 00:11:38,301
us, and it's usually ignored.

208
00:11:38,631 --> 00:11:43,081
We can see where user enters, how far
they get, and where they drop off.

209
00:11:43,681 --> 00:11:47,741
In this example, twenty-eight K entered,
around thirty-seven percentage left

210
00:11:47,741 --> 00:11:52,551
at discovery, another 30% at cart,
and seven percentage at payment.

211
00:11:53,581 --> 00:11:55,661
And here's what most team misses.

212
00:11:56,081 --> 00:11:59,621
The failed journeys matters
as much as successful ones.

213
00:12:00,211 --> 00:12:02,956
A test made only for clean checkout
never touches the abandoned

214
00:12:02,956 --> 00:12:06,801
cart cleanups, payment retries,
other browse and leave traffic.

215
00:12:07,311 --> 00:12:09,251
That actually most of your users do.

216
00:12:09,531 --> 00:12:14,111
So each drop-off becomes a own scenario,
and each one stresses a different part

217
00:12:14,111 --> 00:12:16,491
of your stack, often the weakest part.

218
00:12:17,361 --> 00:12:21,251
If you only test the happy paths, we
are testing the demos, not our users.

219
00:12:23,151 --> 00:12:24,371
So far, this is a method.

220
00:12:24,831 --> 00:12:26,601
Six queries and the logic around them.

221
00:12:27,191 --> 00:12:28,511
The next step is to automate.

222
00:12:28,991 --> 00:12:32,611
The Peak Workload Analyzer is a thin
layer on top of your observability APIs.

223
00:12:33,711 --> 00:12:37,221
Telemetric goes in and comes out
as a dashboard and a JSON report

224
00:12:37,641 --> 00:12:38,801
ready for your load testing.

225
00:12:39,071 --> 00:12:43,511
It's a Java and Spring Boot application
with New Relic and Dynatrace examples,

226
00:12:43,581 --> 00:12:44,991
but the method is vendor neutral

227
00:12:45,991 --> 00:12:50,321
Lastly, the model is small and
a handful of parameters, but the

228
00:12:50,321 --> 00:12:54,101
evidence behind it is not, and
that's what makes it defensible.

229
00:12:54,651 --> 00:12:58,211
Every row here trace back to a query
against the real production traffic.

230
00:12:58,601 --> 00:13:01,151
So when someone challenges a
number, you don't defend your

231
00:13:01,151 --> 00:13:02,801
opinion, you can show a query.

232
00:13:03,471 --> 00:13:04,471
And remember the loop.

233
00:13:04,851 --> 00:13:09,431
After every real peak, rerun these queries
